 # Neural Radiance Fields
+
 Also called: Explicit Radiance Field Representations
 
 ## Overview
-Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and its variants solve the problem of novel view synthesis by representing a 3D scene as a continuous volumetric function that maps spatial coordinates and viewing directions to emitted RGB color and volume density [S1][S5][S10, unofficial]. This approach replaces explicit geometric primitives with an implicit neural representation encoded in network weights, enabling photorealistic rendering of complex scenes with view-dependent effects like specularities and transparency [S1][S2, unofficial][S14, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Neural Volumes](912-neural-volumes.md) by using continuous MLPs or hybrid grids rather than fixed 3D CNN volumes, and from [3D Gaussian Splatting](1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) by relying on differentiable volume integration along rays rather than explicit splatting of Gaussian primitives.
+Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) represent a 3D scene as a continuous volumetric function that maps 3D spatial coordinates and 2D viewing directions to emitted RGB color and volume density [S1][S5][S10, unofficial]. It solves the problem of novel view synthesis by replacing explicit geometric primitives (meshes or point clouds) with an implicit neural representation encoded in network weights, enabling photorealistic rendering of complex scenes with view-dependent effects like specularities and transparency [S1][S2, unofficial][S14, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from its parent method [Radiance-field optimization](44-radiance-field-optimization.md) by introducing a specific asymmetric MLP architecture that injects viewing direction near the output layer, and a two-stage hierarchical sampling strategy (coarse and fine networks) to efficiently allocate samples to opaque regions [S2, unofficial][S4][S7, unofficial].
 
